/**
@example Hello.ino
The smallest complete CHGame sketch (the CHGame library's `examples/Hello`).

Steer the ball with the D-pad. A chirps and counts; the count is saved in
flash and is still there after a power cycle. B re-dyes the felt. It shows
the frame loop every CHGame game uses: logic, then `gfx_wait()`, then
drawing into the framebuffer, then `gfx_flushAsync()`.

The twenty casino games in the library's `examples/Games/` folder are
complete games built the same way.
*/

/**
@example HelloGraphics.ino
The smallest useful CHGfx sketch: draw into the framebuffer, then send the
whole frame to the panel by DMA in one burst.
*/

/**
@example PartialUpdate.ino
Sending only the rectangle that changed (`gfx_flushRect()`): the cost of a
flush scales with its area.
*/

/**
@example GameKit.ino
CHGfx's game-making pieces on one screen: a clip rectangle under a HUD,
span sprites with palette remaps and rotation, row operations, shapes,
outlined banner text and palette animation.
*/

/**
@example Fonts.ino
Custom bitmap fonts: the bundled fonts and Adafruit_GFX's, and the one
gotcha (a custom font's y is its baseline).
*/

/**
@example Demoscene.ino
A seven-part demo that uses both rendering modes: the 16-colour
framebuffer, and `gfx_stream()` computing full-colour pixels straight into
the DMA's buffer.
*/

/**
@example AdafruitGFXCompat.ino
Keep Adafruit_GFX drawing code and put CHGfx's framebuffer and DMA
transport underneath it (`CHGfx_GFX`).
*/

/**
@example Benchmark.ino
The graphics throughput benchmark: every primitive and flush mode timed on
the board, with results on the serial monitor and the panel.
*/
